package local_ref_binding_escape_through_out_param_admits;

fn foo<'a>(out: &mut &'a i64) {
    let v: i64 = 1i64;
    let r: &i64 = &v;
    *out = r;     // THE DEFECT: admitted, upstream E0597
}

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row local_ref_binding_escape_through_out_param_admits (tier 2).
// Illegal Rust (E0597: `v` does not live long enough). TODAY compiled clean, while the
// same borrow written in place, `*out = &v;`, IS refused ("stored through 'out'"). The
// through-a-call spelling `let r = id(&v); *out = r;` is admitted the same way.
// The out-param escape arm asks `collect_borrowed_local_roots`, which has no VarRef arm:
// a binding that HOLDS a borrow names nothing.
// ⚠ ROWED, NOT CLOSED — REASON (3), ITS OWN COST IS MEASURED NON-ZERO. The strict
// extension at the same helper — a VarRef arm reading the §B6 source map
// (`ref_sources_under`) — was built and priced as probe `lrvar` (2026-09-12r, build
// 5d215966230faf3c): it refuses THIS program and its call twin, and it REFUSES THE
// LEGAL
//     let h: H<'a> = H { r: p }; let hr: &H<'a> = &h; let e: &'a i64 = hr.r; *out = e;
// with "'h' does not live long enough": the §B6 map names the HOLDER a reference was
